Task: resume-validation-tests + resume-validation-impl (plan agui_hitl_interrupts_a1f3c2d8, phase P4)

Why: spec FR5.

Finding that shaped the fix: writing the tests first showed the
'approved is required only for tool_call' half of FR5 was ALREADY
satisfied, and not by accident. Validation checks the payload against
rec.ResponseSchema, and 'approved' is required only because
buildToolCallInterrupt advertises ToolConfirmationResponseSchema. An
input request carries its own schema or none, so it never inherited the
rule. Six of the seven new tests passed on first run and are
characterization tests documenting that.

The one real gap was scalar payloads: ResumePayloadMap demands a JSON
object, so a node advertising {"type":"string"} had its answer rejected
before reaching ADK.

Changes:
- agui/internal/interrupt/interrupt.go: new validateResolvedPayload,
  called from ValidateResumeAgainstPending in place of the inline
  ResumePayloadMap + ValidatePayloadAgainstSchema pair.
- agui/internal/interrupt/validate_reason_test.go (new): 9 cases.

Validation composition for input requests:
- validateJSONSchemaValue first, which checks the top-level type and, for
  objects, nested property types.
- ValidatePayloadAgainstSchema after, but only for object payloads, since
  'required' is an object-only rule.
This ordering means an object payload contradicting a scalar schema is
caught by the type check rather than slipping through the object path.

Verification: validateResolvedPayload 100%; interrupt package 71.8% ->
84.5% across the track. go fmt, go vet, CI=true go test ./... clean.
